  <h2>Context</h2>
  <p>I contributed payment-processing work within an established ticketing platform. It was a useful example of engineering inside a mature system, where the value comes from fitting a change into behaviour that many other teams and customers already rely on.</p>

  <h2>Working inside an existing system</h2>
  <p>Payment work is rarely isolated. A change needs to fit with existing flows, operational expectations and the people who will investigate an issue later. That makes careful understanding an important part of delivery before implementation begins.</p>
  <p>Rather than treating the platform as a blank slate, the work meant identifying the narrowest useful change, protecting established behaviour and communicating clearly with the people responsible for adjacent areas.</p>

  <h2>My contribution</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Contributed engineering work to payment-related functionality within a larger platform.</li>
    <li>Worked with existing system boundaries and the dependencies around a mature customer journey.</li>
    <li>Approached the change as a collaborative contribution rather than sole ownership of the wider result.</li>
  </ul>

  <aside class="article-callout">
    <p><strong>Clear attribution matters.</strong> This page describes the part of the work I contributed. It does not claim sole credit for the platform, team or its business outcomes.</p>
  </aside>

  <h2>What this illustrates</h2>
  <p>Experienced engineering in an established system is often about judgement: learning the constraints, selecting a careful change and leaving the software easier for the next person to work with.</p>

